* /**
*  * @file GIT.CLI
*  * @version 1.0
*  */
* GIT.CLI — the GIT verb for a STOCK OpenQM / ScarletDME (mv_git#243).
*
* THIS IS A SHIM, AND SAYS SO.  Everywhere else the GIT verb is the real
* implementation -- BP/GIT, the shared one, which under $DEFINE ENGINE calls
* the record-git engine in this very process.  That route needs CCALL to reach
* QM's own dh_ layer, which needs three fixes that are not in stock ScarletDME
* (geneb/ScarletDME#109, #111 and #113).  On a QM that carries them, install.sh
* installs BP/GIT and this file is not used at all.
*
* What is available on a stock QM is qm-git, the CLI, which does the whole job
* through QMClient — a supported API that ships with ScarletDME.  So this shim
* hands the sentence to it and prints what comes back, which gives an account
* `GIT STATUS' at the TCL prompt with no patched engine at all.
*
* IT IS NOT NAMED `GIT', DELIBERATELY.  It is installed AS BP/GIT, but a file
* called GIT sitting in qm/BP/ beside the wrappers means any `cp qm/BP/* BP/'
* silently replaces the real verb with the shim -- which then works, slowly and
* out of session, and looks like the real thing.  The name is the guard.
*
* THE ACCOUNT COMES FROM THE CURRENT DIRECTORY.  A QM session runs in its
* account's directory and qm-git reads the account name from it, so nothing
* has to be passed — and $QMACCOUNT overrides it if a site's directory is
* named differently from its account.
      PROMPT ""
      SENT = TRIM(@SENTENCE)
*     Drop the verb itself, however the user spelled it, and keep the rest of
*     the line EXACTLY as typed: quoting inside it is the caller's, and
*     rebuilding the line from tokens is how `GIT COMMIT -m "two words"' turns
*     into two arguments.
      VERB = FIELD(SENT, " ", 1)
      ARGS = TRIM(SENT[COL2() + 1, 999999])
      IF ARGS = "" THEN ARGS = "--help"
      CMD = "qm-git " : ARGS
      OS.EXECUTE CMD CAPTURING OUT
      RC = STATUS()
      N = DCOUNT(OUT, @AM)
      FOR I = 1 TO N
         CRT OUT<I>
      NEXT I
*     The CLI's exit status is the answer to "did it work"; a verb that always
*     looks successful is one a paragraph cannot test.  OS.EXECUTE takes only
*     CAPTURING here -- no SETTING clause -- so the status comes from STATUS().
      IF RC # 0 THEN
         STOP
      END
      END
